"},{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"Bengt","key":"johanssonpau","last":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Bengt","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","corresponding":null,"first":"Kirsten","key":"björnlingerpau","last":"Björnlinger","name":"Björnlinger, Kirsten","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"10","corresponding":null,"first":"Mihajl","key":"sekepau","last":"Seke","name":"Seke, Mihajl","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11","corresponding":null,"first":"Måns","key":"agruppau","last":"Agrup","name":"Agrup, Måns","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Björn","key":"zackrissonpau","last":"Zackrisson","name":"Zackrisson, Björn","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisabeth","key":"kjellénpau","last":"Kjellén","name":"Kjellén, Elisabeth","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"franzénpau","last":"Franzén","name":"Franzén, Lars","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"widmarkpau","last":"Widmark","name":"Widmark, Anders","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33444529"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/s1470-2045(20)30581-7","issn":["14702045","14745488"],"issn_org":["14702045"],"issue":"2","journal":"The Lancet Oncology","pages":"235-245","publisher":"Elsevier Ltd","status":"p","title":"The Lancet Oncology","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100046913"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-fecbd5c5-e2e0-4aa2-99a9-f4bb38252de2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Ultra-hypofractionated versus conventionally fractionated radiotherapy for prostate cancer (HYPO-RT-PC) : patient-reported quality-of-life outcomes of a randomised, controlled, non-inferiority, phase 3 trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-274736","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"(1) Background: Methyl-group donors (MGDs), including folate, choline, betaine, and methionine, may influence breast cancer (BC) risk through their role in one-carbon metabolism; (2) Methods: We studied the relationship between dietary intakes of MGDs and BC risk, adopting data from the European Prospective Investigation into Cancer and Nutrition (EPIC) cohort; (3) Results: 318,686 pre- and postmenopausal women were followed between enrolment in 1992-2000 and December 2013-December 2015. Dietary MGD intakes were estimated at baseline through food-frequency questionnaires. Multivariable Cox proportional hazards regression models were used to quantify the association between dietary intake of MGDs, measured both as a calculated score based on their sum and individually, and BC risk. Subgroup analyses were performed by hormone receptor status, menopausal status, and level of alcohol intake. During a mean follow-up time of 14.1 years, 13,320 women with malignant BC were identified. No associations were found between dietary intakes of the MGD score or individual MGDs and BC risk. However, a potential U-shaped relationship was observed between dietary folate intake and overall BC risk, suggesting an inverse association for intakes up to 350 mu g/day compared to a reference intake of 205 mu g/day. No statistically significant differences in the associations were observed by hormone receptor status, menopausal status, or level of alcohol intake; (4) Conclusions: There was no strong evidence for an association between MGDs involved in one-carbon metabolism and BC risk. However, a potential U-shaped trend was suggested for dietary folate intake and BC risk. Further research is needed to clarify this association.","author_count":38,"contributors":["Van Puyvelde, Heleen","Papadimitriou, Nikos","Clasen, Joanna","Muller, David","Biessy, Carine","Ferrari, Pietro","Halkjær, Jytte","Overvad, Kim","Tjønneland, Anne","Fortner, Renée T","Katzke, Verena","Schulze, Matthias B","Chiodini, Paolo","Masala, Giovanna","Pala, Valeria","Sacerdote, Carlotta","Tumino, Rosario","Bakker, Marije F","Agudo, Antonio","Ardanaz, Eva","Chirlaque López, María Dolores","Sánchez, Maria-Jose","Ericson, Ulrika","Gylling, Björn","Karlsson, Therese","Manjer, Jonas","Schmidt, Julie A","Nicolas, Geneviève","Casagrande, Corinne","Weiderpass, Elisabete","Heath, Alicia K","Godderis, Lode","Van Herck, Koen","De Bacquer, Dirk","Gunter, Marc J","Huybrechts, Inge"],"cuid":["S02-098.50-030310"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Huybrechts, Inge"],"country":["Belgium","France","Germany","Italy","Netherlands","Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Andalusian School of public health","Azienda Ospedaliera - Universitaria Città della Salute e della Scienza di Torino","Azienda Sanitaria Provinciale (ASP) Ragusa","Biomedical Research Institute of Murcia - Arrixaca","CIBER - Centro de Investigacion Biomedica en Red","Carlos III Health Institute (ISCIII) (CIBER)","Fdn IRCCS Ist Nazl Tumori Milano, Fondazione IRCCS Istituto Nazionale Tumori Milan, Epidemiol & Prevent Unit","Fondazione IRCCS Istituto Nazionale dei Tumori","German Cancer Research Center","German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)","German Institute of Human Nutrition Potsdam-Rehbruecke","Ghent University","IDEWE Externe Dienst Preventie Bescherming Werk","IdiSNA, Navarra Institute for Health Research, Pamplona, Spain.","Imperial College London","Inst Canc Res Prevent & Clin Network ISPRO, Canc Risk Factors & Life Style Epidemiol Unit","Inst Catala Oncologa, Unit Nutr & Canc, Canc Epidemiol Res Program","Institute for the Study and Prevention of Cancer","Instituto de Investigación Biosanitaria ibs","International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC)","International Agency for Research on Cancer (WHO-IARC), Lyon","KU